import GroupApproximation.Sofic.SmallCancellationRouter
import GroupApproximation.Sofic.PeriodicOverlap
import GroupApproximation.Sofic.TorsionDescent

/-!
# The free-group gate half, its two open leaves named as gates, and the router
consequences that mention neither

`Sofic.SmallCancellationRouter` carries the word-level engine of the router as
far as it goes without the classical metric small-cancellation theorem, and
freezes that theorem as `GreendlingerGate α` — consumed as an explicit
hypothesis, never as an axiom.  This file does the same for the two statements
the free-group half still owes, and derives from them everything the router
construction consumes.

## Two gates, no incomplete proofs

`SharpGreendlingerGate` and `TorsionFreeGate` are the two open statements, each
a `Prop` on the alphabet rather than a theorem with a hole.  Every result below
takes the gate it needs as a hypothesis and is fully proved, so this module is
free of incomplete proofs and lands: the open mathematics sits in predicates
nothing in the repository yet inhabits, which is the same discipline
`GreendlingerGate` itself follows and the only one under which a scaffold can
be committed.  When either gate is proved, its consumers lose a hypothesis and
gain nothing else — the unconditional forms are one-line corollaries, and no
signature below moves.

Both gates are stated at the sharp constant where that matters.  At `λ = 1/6`
the sharp arc bound `(1 - 3λ)·|r|` is exactly `|r|/2`, so the half-form falls
out of the sharp gate at one value (`greendlingerConclusion_of_sharpGate`) and
is not a third open statement.

`hlam : lam ≤ 1/6` inside `SharpGreendlingerGate` is not decoration.
Greendlinger's lemma is a theorem about `λ ≤ 1/6` and is false above it — the
curvature count needs every interior face of a reduced diagram to meet at least
seven others, which is what a sixth buys and a fifth does not.  For `λ ≥ 1/3`
the conclusion degenerates, the coefficient being non-positive; the interesting
and genuinely open range is `λ ≤ 1/6`, which is also the only range any
consumer needs, the router family being designed at `1/8`.

`GreendlingerConclusionSharp` is **not defined here.**  It lives beside the
half-form it generalizes, in `SmallCancellationRouter`, and reaches this file
through the `open` below.  Two lanes consume it — this gate half proves it, the
torsion descent spends it — so neither may own it.  Do not reintroduce a local
copy: a definition duplicated between a file's own namespace and an opened one
does not error, the local one silently wins at every call site in this file,
the two constants stay definitionally equal and compose by unfolding, and
nothing breaks until someone edits one body.
-/

namespace GroupApproximation
namespace GreendlingerFreeGate

open SmallCancellationRouter

section Words

variable {α : Type*}

/-! ## 1.  The two open statements, as gates -/

/-- **OPEN — the gate half's own statement.**  Classical metric small
cancellation at its sharp constant: a `C'(λ)` family of cyclically reduced
relators, for `λ` at most a sixth, satisfies the Greendlinger conclusion with
arc bound `(1 - 3λ)·|r|`.

The proof to write is either of the two classical routes, and the repository
has both partly walked: the reduced-diagram curvature count
(`Sofic/Greendlinger*`, residual `CascadeLanding`, whose `i_c = 0` branch is
discharged in `Sofic/GreendlingerDeepestMatch` and
`Sofic/GreendlingerCoincidence`) and Dehn's algorithm as a rewriting system
(`Sofic/GreendlingerDehn*`, residual `PieceOverlapRight`).  Both end at the
same crossing, and it is a van Kampen argument.  Either route produces the
sharp constant directly: `(1 - 3λ)` is what the boundary-layer count gives, and
rounding it to a half is a step taken only for convenience. -/
def SharpGreendlingerGate (α : Type*) : Prop :=
  ∀ (R : Set (List (α × Bool))) (lam : ℚ),
    (∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r) → lam ≤ 1 / 6 →
    MetricSmallCancellation R lam → GreendlingerConclusionSharp R lam

/-- **No relator is a proper power**, at the level of *words*: no relator is
some shorter word written down `m ≥ 2` times.

Without this the torsion gate below would be false, and its failure is
standard: the `C'(1/6)` family generated by `(ab)^7` has no pieces at all —
distinct rotations of `(ab)^7` begin with different letters, and so do the
inverses, so the metric condition holds vacuously — while the image of `ab` in
the quotient has order seven.

The predicate itself is `PeriodicOverlap.IsProperPower`, taken from the
periodicity lane rather than restated here, so that its closure lemmas apply to
this hypothesis with no adapter.  Two choices are recorded, because each could
have gone the other way.

* **Words, not group elements.**  The alternative, `FreeGroup.mk r ≠ s ^ k` for
  every `s` and every `k ≥ 2`, is a *stronger* hypothesis —
  `PeriodicOverlap.exists_pow_of_isProperPower` turns a word-level power into a
  group-level one, and `noProperPower_of_forall_ne_pow` is that implication
  packaged for callers who happen to have the group-level form.  For cyclically
  reduced relators the two agree, since conjugate cyclically reduced words are
  cyclic rotations of one another and a rotation of `uᵐ` is `(rotation of u)ᵐ`.
  The word-level form is primary because it is what the periodicity step
  *produces*: a `List.replicate`/`flatten` decomposition, not a group equation.
  The converse fails — cancellation can make a reduced word that is no block
  power spell a group power — so the two readings are genuinely different
  hypotheses and only one of them is checkable by inspection.
* **On the base family `R`, not on `symmetrization R`.**  This makes the gate
  stronger and the hypothesis checkable against the finitely many relators a
  router construction actually writes down.  It costs the discharge nothing:
  `NoProperPower.mem_symmetrization` below carries the hypothesis to every
  member of `symmetrization R` in one step.  Neither rotation nor formal
  inversion can create or destroy a block power — both are invertible on words,
  and `FreeGroup.invRev` reverses the block order of a power whose blocks are
  all equal, so the exponent is untouched. -/
def NoProperPower (R : Set (List (α × Bool))) : Prop :=
  ∀ r ∈ R, ¬ PeriodicOverlap.IsProperPower r

/-- **CLOSED — the torsion companion.**  The classical small-cancellation
torsion theorem: a metric small-cancellation presentation by cyclically reduced
relators, none of them a proper power, presents a torsion-free group.

This is no longer an open statement.  `torsionFreeGate_of_sharpGate` below
proves it from `SharpGreendlingerGate` alone, the mathematics living in
`Sofic/TorsionDescent` (`isPowerTorsionFree_of_sharp`).  It is kept as a named
predicate because consumers were written against the name and because it
records exactly what the torsion half costs: the sharp gate and nothing else.

**Two restatements against the version that was open here**, both forced.

* **The constant is `λ ≤ 1/8`, not `1/6`.**  The descent's residual branch —
  arc longer than one period, the two rotations distinct — is *satisfiable* at
  `1/6` read through the half-form, so no argument closes it there; at
  `λ ≤ 1/8` read through the sharp form it is arithmetically empty.  Both
  halves of that are machine-checked in `TorsionDescent`
  (`half_form_residual_satisfiable`, `residual_empty_of_lam_le_eighth`).  This
  is why the avatar family is designed at `C'(1/8)`.
* **`hcyc` is still here, but the descent does not use it.**  It is needed only
  to invoke `SharpGreendlingerGate`, which asks for it.  Anything taking the
  sharp *conclusion* directly needs no such hypothesis —
  `TorsionDescent.isPowerTorsionFree_of_sharp` has none.

The old sketch of the proof is now the module `Sofic/TorsionDescent`; two
warnings from it are worth keeping in view here, because both were wrong in an
earlier version of this docstring and both were refuted by machine check rather
than by argument.  The piece bound in the distinct-rotations branch bounds *the
overhang*, not the arc, so it does not on its own contradict the arc being
long.  And the natural-looking sub-lemma "a word sharing more than half of
itself with its own nontrivial rotation is a proper power" is FALSE
(`PeriodicOverlap.not_isProperPower_of_two_mul_length_le`, witness
`0 1 0 0 1 0 0 1`); Fine--Wilf is available and is the wrong tool. -/
def TorsionFreeGate (α : Type*) : Prop :=
  ∀ (R : Set (List (α × Bool))) (lam : ℚ),
    (∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r) → lam ≤ 1 / 8 →
    MetricSmallCancellation R lam → NoProperPower R →
    IsPowerTorsionFree
      (FreeGroup α ⧸ Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R))

/-! ## 2.  The no-proper-power hypothesis, and where it travels -/

/-- The group-level reading of the hypothesis implies the word-level one, so a
caller who knows that no relator is a proper power *in the free group* has the
hypothesis this file asks for. -/
theorem noProperPower_of_forall_ne_pow {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (h : ∀ r ∈ R, ∀ (s : FreeGroup α) (k : ℕ), 2 ≤ k → FreeGroup.mk r ≠ s ^ k) :
    NoProperPower R := by
  intro r hr hpp
  obtain ⟨g, m, hm, hmk⟩ := PeriodicOverlap.exists_pow_of_isProperPower hpp
  exact h r hr g m hm hmk

/-- **The hypothesis transfers to the symmetrization**, which is where the
Greendlinger arc's relator actually lives: the arc is more than half of a
*symmetrized* relator, while `NoProperPower` is stated on the base family.
Written for dot notation, so a discharge reads `hnpp.mem_symmetrization hw`.

Nothing is proved here — `PeriodicOverlap.not_isProperPower_of_mem_symmetrization`
is the content, and `symmetrization R` is *definitionally* the existential it
asks for, so the two compose with no conversion.  The theorem exists to check
that composition against this file's `NoProperPower` and this file's
`symmetrization`, rather than leaving the discharge to discover whether the
interfaces meet. -/
theorem NoProperPower.mem_symmetrization {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (hnpp : NoProperPower R) {w : List (α × Bool)}
    (hw : w ∈ symmetrization R) : ¬ PeriodicOverlap.IsProperPower w :=
  PeriodicOverlap.not_isProperPower_of_mem_symmetrization hnpp hw

/-! ## 3.  The torsion gate, discharged -/

/-- The metric condition is monotone in its constant: a smaller `λ` is a
stronger hypothesis.  Needed because the torsion half is stated at `λ ≤ 1/8`
while the router's own gate is fixed at `1/6`. -/
theorem metricSmallCancellation_mono {R : Set (List (α × Bool))} {lam lam' : ℚ}
    (h : MetricSmallCancellation R lam) (hle : lam ≤ lam') :
    MetricSmallCancellation R lam' := by
  intro p hp w hw hpw
  have hbound := h p hp w hw hpw
  have hw0 : (0 : ℚ) ≤ (w.length : ℚ) := Nat.cast_nonneg _
  have hmul : lam * (w.length : ℚ) ≤ lam' * (w.length : ℚ) :=
    mul_le_mul_of_nonneg_right hle hw0
  linarith

/-- **The torsion gate follows from the sharp gate.**  The whole torsion half of
the router's obligations costs one hypothesis, not two.

The sharp conclusion at `λ` is exactly what `TorsionDescent`'s descent consumes,
and `λ ≤ 1/8` is what empties its residual branch; `hcyc` is spent invoking the
gate and nowhere else. -/
theorem torsionFreeGate_of_sharpGate (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α) :
    TorsionFreeGate α := fun R lam hcyc hlam hmetric hnpp =>
  TorsionDescent.isPowerTorsionFree_of_sharp hlam
    (hgate R lam hcyc (by linarith) hmetric) hmetric hnpp

/-- **Torsion-freeness with no gate hypothesis on the torsion side at all**, in
the shape a consumer holding only the sharp conclusion wants: no `hcyc`, no
gate, just the conclusion the gate would have produced. -/
theorem isPowerTorsionFree_of_sharpConclusion {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    {lam : ℚ} (hlam : lam ≤ 1 / 8)
    (hsharp : GreendlingerConclusionSharp R lam)
    (hmetric : MetricSmallCancellation R lam) (hnpp : NoProperPower R) :
    IsPowerTorsionFree
      (FreeGroup α ⧸ Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R)) :=
  TorsionDescent.isPowerTorsionFree_of_sharp hlam hsharp hmetric hnpp

/-! ## 4.  Everything the router consumes, from the one remaining gate -/

/-- **The half-form, from the sharp gate.**  At `λ = 1/6` the sharp coefficient
is exactly `1/2`, so this is the sharp gate read at one value and cast back to
`ℕ` — not a third open statement. -/
theorem greendlingerConclusion_of_sharpGate (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α)
    {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (hcyc : ∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r)
    (hmetric : MetricSmallCancellation R (1 / 6)) :
    GreendlingerConclusion R := by
  intro w hred hne hmem
  obtain ⟨r, hr, u, huw, hur, hlt⟩ :=
    hgate R (1 / 6) hcyc le_rfl hmetric w hred hne hmem
  refine ⟨r, hr, u, huw, hur, ?_⟩
  have h2 : (r.length : ℚ) < 2 * (u.length : ℚ) := by
    norm_num at hlt
    linarith
  exact_mod_cast h2

/-- The router's own gate, from the sharp one: the same statement with its
quantifier restored and the constant fixed. -/
theorem greendlingerGate_of_sharpGate (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α) :
    GreendlingerGate α :=
  fun _ hcyc hmetric => greendlingerConclusion_of_sharpGate hgate hcyc hmetric

/-- **Nontrivial elements of the relator subgroup are longer than half of some
symmetrized relator**, from the sharp gate and the metric condition. -/
theorem norm_bound_of_sharpGate [DecidableEq α]
    (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α) {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (hcyc : ∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r)
    (hmetric : MetricSmallCancellation R (1 / 6))
    {g : FreeGroup α} (hg : g ≠ 1)
    (hmem : g ∈ Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R)) :
    ∃ r ∈ symmetrization R, r.length < 2 * FreeGroup.norm g :=
  norm_bound_of_greendlinger
    (greendlingerConclusion_of_sharpGate hgate hcyc hmetric) hg hmem

/-- **The router's protected-set obligation.**  A `C'(1/6)` presentation is
injective on every protected set whose pairwise differences are short against
all symmetrized relators — which is the field `RoutingLemmaData.protected_injOn`
asks for, and from which nontriviality of the routed protected element is
derived rather than stored. -/
theorem protected_injOn_of_sharpGate [DecidableEq α]
    (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α) {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (hcyc : ∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r)
    (hmetric : MetricSmallCancellation R (1 / 6))
    (P : Set (FreeGroup α))
    (hshort : ∀ r ∈ symmetrization R, ∀ x ∈ P, ∀ y ∈ P,
      2 * FreeGroup.norm (x / y) ≤ r.length) :
    Set.InjOn
      (QuotientGroup.mk' (Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R)))
      P :=
  protected_injOn_of_gate (greendlingerGate_of_sharpGate hgate) hcyc hmetric
    P hshort

/-- **The free-group consumption point.**  Both word-combinatorial fields of
`RoutingLemmaData` — injectivity on the protected set and torsion-freeness of
the quotient — from the two gates and one hypothesis list on the relator
family.

This is the theorem the router construction calls.  When the gates are proved,
the two hypotheses disappear from this signature and nothing else changes. -/
theorem router_conclusions_of_gates [DecidableEq α]
    (hgate : SharpGreendlingerGate α) (htors : TorsionFreeGate α)
    {R : Set (List (α × Bool))}
    (hcyc : ∀ r ∈ R, FreeGroup.IsCyclicallyReduced r)
    (hmetric : MetricSmallCancellation R (1 / 6)) (hnpp : NoProperPower R)
    (P : Set (FreeGroup α))
    (hshort : ∀ r ∈ symmetrization R, ∀ x ∈ P, ∀ y ∈ P,
      2 * FreeGroup.norm (x / y) ≤ r.length) :
    Set.InjOn
        (QuotientGroup.mk' (Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R))) P ∧
      IsPowerTorsionFree
        (FreeGroup α ⧸ Subgroup.normalClosure (FreeGroup.mk '' R)) :=
  ⟨protected_injOn_of_sharpGate hgate hcyc hmetric P hshort,
    htors R hcyc hmetric hnpp⟩

end Words

end GreendlingerFreeGate
end GroupApproximation
